    {
      "meaning": "New beginning",
      "description": "The number eight symbolizes a new beginning and new life. Circumcision was performed on the eighth day as a sign of the covenant. Eight people were saved through the floodwaters on Noah's ark. In Christian tradition, the number is linked to the resurrection, which took place on the first day of a new week—the eighth day.",
      "reason": "[minor] The description is good and theologically correct, but there are interesting additional facts that could enrich the entry through footnotes. Among these are the kabbalistic/rabbinic connection between the number 8 and the letter het (ח) with its numerical value of 8, as well as the historical fact that many early Christian baptismal chapels (baptisteries) were built in an octagonal shape to symbolize a new beginning.; [minor] The phrasing \"the first day of a new week — the eighth day\" is correct, but could be clarified slightly to make the connection between the seventh day (the Sabbath) and the eighth day more evident.",
      "score": 8,
      "date": "2026-04-04"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "The Hebrew letter het (ח) has the numerical value 8 and is associated with life (חַיִּים, chayyim), which underscores the connection between eight and new life.",
      "source": "kabbalistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Many early Christian baptisteries were built with an octagonal floor plan to symbolize that baptism is a new beginning—linked to the eighth day and the resurrection.",
      "source": "historisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Church Father Augustine and other early theologians referred to Sunday as \"the eighth day\" (dies octava), a day that transcends the earthly weekly cycle and points toward eternal life.",
      "source": "teologisk"
    }
  ]
